1
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:16,300
Enriched uranium, one of man's most valuable resources.

2
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:47,199
This enriched uranium button weighs 5 kilograms, 11 pounds.

3
00:00:47,299 --> 00:00:51,999
Its value, $55,000.

4
00:00:52,099 --> 00:00:58,300
These buttons will be alloyed, cast, rolled, or machined to make the various forms and

5
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:05,120
shapes useful to industry, science and defense. Because enriched uranium is

6
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:11,260
worth almost ten times its weight in gold, the many forms of salvage and waste

7
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:16,660
products will be recycled again and again to recover the last salvageable

8
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:22,900
quantity of enriched uranium. Throughout these operations certain basic

9
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,059
principles of nuclear safety will be followed to prevent accidents caused by

10
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:45,079
the phenomenon called criticality.

11
00:01:45,179 --> 00:01:51,540
Today most people find no strangeness in our concept that all matter is made up

12
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:59,919
of atoms. Natural uranium is a mixture of two kinds of metallic atoms, uranium-238

13
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:08,560
and uranium-235. Most of these atoms are relatively stable uranium-238 or more

14
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:16,600
simply U-238. Only one of every 140 natural uranium atoms is of the more

15
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:24,280
excitable U-235. Both metals look the same. In fact, they can't be chemically separated.

16
00:02:25,339 --> 00:02:31,440
Now, if one of the tiny wandering particles that we call neutrons happens to collide with an atom

17
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:40,280
of U-238, it may simply bounce off or it may be captured or absorbed into the atom. If, on the

18
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:46,520
other hand the neutron happens to hit a u-235 atom it may split or fission the

19
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:52,780
atom into several fragments part of the fissioning atom turns into pure energy

20
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:59,720
most of it appearing as heat or in the form of powerful x-rays in this release

21
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:06,840
of energy atomic power is born the leftover pieces of the broken atom are

22
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:13,940
called fission products. They are intensely radioactive and are now entirely different

23
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:22,080
elements of lighter weight, no longer uranium of any kind. A very important part of the fission

24
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:28,660
process is that the breaking atom throws out two or three of its own neutrons, each of which may

25
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:35,480
in turn hit and fission still another atom of U-235. Thus we may obtain a

26
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:41,799
constantly increasing number of fissions, a self-sustaining chain reaction,

27
00:03:41,899 --> 00:03:48,880
criticality. We can put criticality to work in a nuclear reactor. Excess

28
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:54,139
neutrons not required to maintain criticality may be directed at special

29
00:03:54,239 --> 00:04:01,299
targets and if captured transform them into radioisotopes used in medicine,

30
00:04:01,399 --> 00:04:09,419
agriculture, industry and many types of research.

31
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:20,499
Heat emitted during a chain reaction can be used to generate electricity to power

32
00:04:20,599 --> 00:04:29,359
large cities, desalt the seas or power submarines.

33
00:04:30,999 --> 00:04:34,959
There is another side to criticality.

34
00:04:41,999 --> 00:04:47,579
Nuclear explosives have constructive applications such as digging canals and

35
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:51,919
and inland waterways, recovering valuable mineral deposits,

36
00:04:52,019 --> 00:04:55,880
and creating new exotic elements.

37
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:59,760
The ability of the U-235 atom to fission

38
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:03,840
has opened a limitless future to mankind.

39
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:07,400
Like fire and electricity, this new tool

40
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,900
must be properly controlled.

41
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,640
Through the mining and milling stages, uranium can be handled very much like any other valuable metal,

42
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:20,480
Although slightly radioactive, it can be touched without the use of gloves.

43
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:25,260
Some operations, which involve extensive handling of uranium, however,

44
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:28,800
may require the use of gloves or respirators.

45
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:34,060
Following the milling operation, uranium is put through a processing step

46
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:36,440
which no other metal undergoes.

47
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:40,260
This process, known as gaseous diffusion,

48
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:46,460
separates the U-238 atoms from the fissionable U-235 atoms.

49
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:51,760
The uranium in the form of gaseous uranium hexafluoride is pumped through

50
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:57,620
specially constructed porous barriers. The end product is uranium hexafluoride

51
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:04,560
highly enriched in U-235. Later this gas will be converted to powder or to metal.

52
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:09,420
Once uranium has been enriched in U-235 it must be handled in a strictly

53
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:15,260
prescribed manner to prevent accidental criticality that can cause injury or

54
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:21,200
or death personnel must avoid assembling a critical mass that is the amount of you

55
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:29,140
to 35 necessary to sustain a chain reaction this amount will differ according to the shape

56
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:37,780
or geometry of the u-2 35 in addition to certain other factors when criticality occurs gamma

57
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:44,000
radiation and neutrons are emitted which can cause injuries to personnel ranging from flesh

58
00:06:44,099 --> 00:06:50,419
burns to the damage of internal organs. Although such accidents are extremely

59
00:06:50,519 --> 00:06:57,319
rare, they can occur if safety rules are not followed. These rules are derived

60
00:06:57,419 --> 00:07:01,699
from experiments with uranium assemblies conducted in special criticality

61
00:07:01,799 --> 00:07:04,699
laboratories.

62
00:07:14,099 --> 00:07:21,819
Data obtained in experiments assist in determining the safety limits for handling or storing

63
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:25,980
certain enrichments, quantities or shapes of uranium.

64
00:07:44,099 --> 00:07:54,980
Such information is used by nuclear safety engineers to make the practical decisions regarding the moving or storing of enriched uranium within a nuclear plant.

65
00:07:56,140 --> 00:08:05,239
Although rules and procedures may vary slightly at different installations, certain basic principles of nuclear safety are always valid.

66
00:08:05,339 --> 00:08:14,639
The first principle, awareness of the degree of enrichment, the percentage of U-235 contained

67
00:08:14,739 --> 00:08:18,299
in any unit of uranium.

68
00:08:18,399 --> 00:08:24,340
The higher the percentage of U-235 within any batch of uranium, the lower the quantity

69
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:29,100
of uranium that may be safely placed within a given space.

70
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:37,800
For instance, uranium enriched to only 2% U-235 would not reach the danger stage until it filled a drum.

71
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:50,080
On the other hand, uranium with a 50% enrichment of U-235 under optimum conditions could reach the critical stage with only two cups of material.

72
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:58,600
At a 90 percent enrichment, under optimum conditions, only one cup of U-235 would be

73
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:02,200
required for criticality.

74
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:07,999
The second principle for nuclear safety is the control of the quantity or mass of enriched

75
00:09:08,099 --> 00:09:11,379
uranium within a given space.

76
00:09:11,479 --> 00:09:17,759
This control is subject to such factors as the volume and shape of containers, the concentration

77
00:09:17,859 --> 00:09:27,720
of U-235 in mixed materials and interaction. Since handling large quantities of U-235 increases

78
00:09:27,820 --> 00:09:33,399
the risk of accidental criticality. Quantity can be controlled in some operations by the

79
00:09:33,499 --> 00:09:41,159
use of small volume containers. But if certain operations require the movement or storage

80
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:50,080
of large quantities of U-235, criticality can be avoided by the use of special shapes.

81
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:56,160
In the thin container, the uranium is spread out. Neutrons are more likely to escape the

82
00:09:56,260 --> 00:10:03,600
boundaries of the containers without coming into contact with U-235 atoms.

83
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:09,520
In the fat container, the U-235 atoms are massed, thereby increasing the chance that

84
00:10:09,619 --> 00:10:16,980
neutrons within the mass will be trapped and set off a chain reaction. These unusual looking

85
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:24,299
containers are used for storage of various solutions of U-235. Their small diameter makes

86
00:10:24,399 --> 00:10:32,359
them nuclear safe. These pipes contain many gallons of solutions rich in U-235, but their

87
00:10:32,459 --> 00:10:41,960
shape prevents the U-235 from assembling a critical mass. When U-235 is mixed with other

88
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:49,039
materials, the concentration of U-235 becomes important in maintaining criticality control.

89
00:10:49,139 --> 00:10:57,879
Very often U-235 in a mixture with other materials is potentially more dangerous than U-235 in pure

90
00:10:57,979 --> 00:11:03,700
concentration. The reason is that some materials such as water or the various

91
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:11,220
forms of carbon tend to slow or moderate the speed of neutrons. When neutrons are

92
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:17,639
moving at slower speeds, they are more readily trapped by U-235 atoms, a

93
00:11:17,739 --> 00:11:25,119
situation which can lead to criticality. If U-235 is mixed with the material

94
00:11:25,219 --> 00:11:29,980
tends to moderate neutrons, there is usually an optimum concentration point

95
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:36,179
at which criticality is most likely to occur. While below or above this point,

96
00:11:36,279 --> 00:11:42,960
criticality would be less likely to occur. Personnel handling enriched

97
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:49,499
uranium must always be aware of the possibility of interaction. Interaction

98
00:11:49,599 --> 00:11:55,060
can occur when neutrons escaping from one batch of uranium cause fissions in a

99
00:11:55,160 --> 00:12:00,739
neighboring match this can be avoided by allowing sufficient distance between

100
00:12:00,839 --> 00:12:08,340
individual containers of uranium to allow neutrons to escape the birdcage

101
00:12:08,440 --> 00:12:13,000
container is designed and constructed to provide sufficient spacing between

102
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:19,180
uranium masses but even the birdcage spacing system has its limitations

103
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:23,739
containers should never be stacked above the height limit designated for each

104
00:12:23,839 --> 00:12:29,919
storage area. Proper distances between authorized storage areas also will

105
00:12:30,019 --> 00:12:38,259
prevent interaction. Proper spacing on vehicles, elevators, and transport dollies

106
00:12:38,359 --> 00:12:45,159
must be observed to prevent criticality caused by interaction. Another important

107
00:12:45,259 --> 00:12:51,659
principle is the control of neutron reflection. When neutrons escape from a

108
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:57,900
fission atom. They tend to bounce about until their energy is spent and they are captured

109
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:06,180
by other atoms. Certain materials placed near a mass of U-235 tend to act as neutron reflectors

110
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:14,780
which bounce the neutrons back into the U-235 mass and cause more fissions. A wall, a ceiling,

111
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:20,380
a drum of water, or even persons can reflect neutrons.

112
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:29,100
When a reflecting material is present, less U-235 is required to cause criticality.

113
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:35,960
A final principle for criticality control is the use of neutron absorbers.

114
00:13:36,060 --> 00:13:41,960
Neutron absorbers are materials which remove neutrons from circulation and prevent them

115
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:45,340
from causing further fissions.

116
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:50,700
Elements such as boron and cadmium are effective neutron absorbers and may

117
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:55,460
be used to prevent or to stop criticality.

118
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:59,239
Again, the nuclear safety principles.

119
00:13:59,339 --> 00:14:02,519
Awareness of degree of enrichment.

120
00:14:02,619 --> 00:14:06,999
Control of mass or quantity through the control of volume,

121
00:14:07,099 --> 00:14:10,759
shape, concentration, and interaction.

122
00:14:10,859 --> 00:14:18,679
control of neutron reflection, and use of neutron absorbers.

123
00:14:18,779 --> 00:14:22,759
These metal buttons, highly enriched in U-235,

124
00:14:22,859 --> 00:14:26,600
are about to begin a typical production cycle.

125
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:32,779
They are stored in properly spaced trays to prevent the assembly of a critical mass.

126
00:14:32,879 --> 00:14:40,039
When enriched uranium is being moved within a building, it is transported over an approved route.

127
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:43,400
When U-235 is moved by truck, load limits

128
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:48,960
are strictly observed to prevent assembly of a critical mass.

129
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:52,340
If the U-235 is being transported from one building

130
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:59,420
to another within a plant, the driver goes directly to his destination without stopping or parking

131
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:01,480
en route.

132
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:04,840
The U-235 should never be stored at a dock,

133
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:09,780
but should be taken directly to an authorized holding area.

134
00:15:09,880 --> 00:15:15,940
When enriched uranium metal is machined, special care is taken to prevent the loss of valuable chips

135
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:21,320
and shavings, and to prevent the assembly of a critical mass.

136
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:29,480
The chips remain in containers on a special rack at the workstation until they are collected

137
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:35,720
and emptied into a nuclear safe container half filled with the neutron absorber perchloroethylene.

138
00:15:43,979 --> 00:15:49,340
Eventually, these chips will be moved to another location for cleaning and return

139
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:52,759
to the production cycle.

140
00:15:52,859 --> 00:15:58,279
During the dimensional inspection of enriched uranium components, only one component at a time

141
00:15:58,379 --> 00:16:01,119
is permitted on an inspection table.

142
00:16:01,219 --> 00:16:07,419
The next component to be inspected waits its turn in an authorized holding area.

143
00:16:07,519 --> 00:16:11,799
In some inspection areas where other types of metals share the inspection equipment with

144
00:16:11,899 --> 00:16:19,560
enriched uranium, special provisions are required to prevent possible misidentification.

145
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:27,799
In the radiography room, safe loading procedure is used to prevent a critical mass from forming.

146
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:31,800
The new 235 components are always well separated.

147
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:11,220
In a typical packaging operation, U-235 components are wrapped in plastic, placed in a can which

148
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:20,039
then is sealed, and loaded into a foam glass shipping container approved for interstate

149
00:17:20,139 --> 00:17:36,159
transport.

150
00:17:36,259 --> 00:17:43,899
Throughout a production cycle, many types of U-235 waste products are generated.

151
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:54,239
These chips and shavings are cleaned and bracheted for reentry into the production system.

152
00:17:54,339 --> 00:18:04,019
Mop water, filters, and combustibles all contain tiny, but nonetheless valuable quantities

153
00:18:04,119 --> 00:18:07,700
of U-235.

154
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:15,400
Solutions from this and other liquid salvage operations are stored in nuclear safe containers.

155
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:21,239
Eventually these solutions will be fed into a central uranium recovery system consisting

156
00:18:21,339 --> 00:18:24,680
of extraction columns.

157
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:31,860
After passing through the columns, the uranium is evaporated and converted to uranium trioxide,

158
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:36,860
to uranium tetrafluoride and reentry into a production cycle.

159
00:18:37,880 --> 00:18:43,420
In all production and recovery areas, gamma radiation detection systems are installed

160
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:45,700
for the protection of personnel.

161
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:50,460
If the radiation level should rise because of an accidental criticality,

162
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:53,100
an alarm system is activated.

163
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:57,860
In an emergency, personnel would evacuate a building by well-marked routes

164
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:01,940
and regroup at designated assembly points.

165
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:06,620
If radiation contamination is suspected,

166
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:09,500
persons are monitored by health physicists.

167
00:19:11,340 --> 00:19:14,980
Other post-accident aids for determining extent of radiation levels

168
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:19,220
include special materials inside the personnel identification badges

169
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:23,540
and dosimeters located in work areas.

170
00:19:23,639 --> 00:19:31,399
In some installations, criticality control is made more complex because of the presence of

171
00:19:31,499 --> 00:19:46,759
uranium-233, a uranium isotope produced in a nuclear reactor. U-233 requires stricter handling

172
00:19:46,859 --> 00:19:54,999
procedures than U-235 because less U-233 is required for criticality, and because it is

173
00:19:55,099 --> 00:20:00,440
often associated with highly radioactive products.

174
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:07,499
Some installations handle plutonium, also a nuclear reactor product.

175
00:20:07,599 --> 00:20:14,720
Plutonium is more highly fissionable than either U-235 or U-233, and has a high alpha

176
00:20:14,820 --> 00:20:18,120
radiation activity that requires special facilities

177
00:20:18,220 --> 00:20:19,960
for handling.

178
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:23,739
The handling of fissionable materials such as U-235,

179
00:20:23,839 --> 00:20:27,499
U-233, and the isotopes of plutonium

180
00:20:27,599 --> 00:20:30,960
requires a constant alertness on the part of personnel

181
00:20:31,060 --> 00:20:34,440
to prevent criticality accidents.

182
00:20:34,540 --> 00:20:40,039
Workmen in the enriched uranium processing industry have established an enviable safety record

183
00:20:40,139 --> 00:20:44,660
by adhering to established rules for nuclear safety.

184
00:20:44,760 --> 00:20:48,700
As the need for nuclear power continues to grow,

185
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:52,100
and as national defense commitments continue,

186
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:55,560
more and more enriched uranium will be processed

187
00:20:55,660 --> 00:20:58,220
in production plants.

188
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:03,380
It is the responsibility of every person employed in the processing and fabrication

189
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:11,460
of enriched uranium to prevent the waste of this valuable resource and to maintain the excellent nuclear

190
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:13,499
safety record.
